                        }
                    },
                    "comment": "t.maximum - the larger of two tensors, element by element"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 921,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 841,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Compares two tensors element by element and keeps the LARGER value at every position.\n\nWhere t.clamp has one floor for the whole tensor, t.maximum's floor is another tensor, so it can be different at every position: a per-element lower bound, a noise floor that varies across a spectrum, or simply the upper envelope of two signals. With a single number in tensor b it is a plain floor, the same everywhere (t.maximum against 0 keeps only the positive part of a signal).\n\nTHE NAME AND WHAT IT DOES:\nt.maximum does not find the largest value in a tensor - that is t.max, the reduction. It takes the larger of two values, one pair at a time, which keeps the result at or ABOVE tensor b: a floor.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.maximum\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe first tensor (a list or number is turned into a float32 tensor). Receiving it makes the node compare and send.\n\ntensor b in:\nThe second tensor, or a single number used everywhere. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing. It is converted to tensor a's type and device, so against an integer tensor a b of 0.5 becomes 0.\n\nThe two shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one of them has length 1. Shapes that cannot be matched raise an error and send nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmaximum tensor:\nThe larger of a and b at every position, in the broadcast shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe plot draws the result over the two inputs: the sine (a) and a ramp from -1 to 1 (b). Wherever the sine is above the ramp the result follows the sine; wherever it dips below, the result rides along the ramp. So on the left, where the ramp is low, almost all of the wave survives, and on the right only the peaks that rise above the ramp are left. Drag the ramp's start and stop (try both 0: a flat floor at 0 that cuts off every trough).\n\nRELATED:\nt.minimum keeps the smaller value instead. t.clamp limits both sides with single numbers. t.max finds the largest value in one tensor. np.max and max do the same floor for NumPy arrays and numbers.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 825,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Compares two tensors element by element and keeps the LARGER value at every position.\n\nWhere t.clamp has one floor for the whole tensor, t.maximum's floor is another tensor, so it can be different at every position: a per-element lower bound, a noise floor that varies across a spectrum, or simply the upper envelope of two signals. With a single number in tensor b it is a plain floor, the same everywhere (t.maximum against 0 keeps only the positive part of a signal).\n\nTHE NAME AND WHAT IT DOES:\nt.maximum does not find the largest value in a tensor - that is t.max, the reduction. It takes the larger of two values, one pair at a time, which keeps the result at or ABOVE tensor b: a floor.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.maximum\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe first tensor (a list or number is turned into a float32 tensor). Receiving it makes the node compare and send.\n\ntensor b in:\nThe second tensor, or a single number used everywhere. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing. It is converted to tensor a's type and device, so against an integer tensor a b of 0.5 becomes 0.\n\nThe two shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one of them has length 1. Shapes that cannot be matched raise an error and send nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmaximum tensor:\nThe larger of a and b at every position, in the broadcast shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe plot draws the result over the two inputs: the sine (a) and a ramp from -1 to 1 (b). Wherever the sine is above the ramp the result follows the sine; wherever it dips below, the result rides along the ramp. So on the left, where the ramp is low, almost all of the wave survives, and on the right only the peaks that rise above the ramp are left. Drag the ramp's start and stop (try both 0: a flat floor at 0 that cuts off every trough).\n\nRELATED:\nt.minimum keeps the smaller value instead. t.clamp limits both sides with single numbers. t.max finds the largest value in one tensor. np.max and max do the same floor for NumPy arrays and numbers."
                },
